WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:10.019
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I now turn to item seven
on the agenda, which is the NHS performance

00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:15.639
report. This is a regular report to the Board,
and I invite Barbara Hakin to introduce it.

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:21.500
&gt;&gt;Barbara Hakin: Thank you, Chairman. This
month's report includes a section on the outcomes

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:29.340
framework, as well as the standards on which
I normally report. What I would like to do,

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.860
as ever, rather than going through the report,
which I know Board members will have read,

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:39.200
is to pick out one or two areas where I want
to talk about some of the issues that we are

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:40.940
facing and how we are dealing with them.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:48.000
I would like to start with referral to treatment,
which, as Board members know, is the standard

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.680
for patients who are referred to outpatients
and then have diagnostic tests, and their

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:59.850
treatment is the standard for patients who
are not admitted urgently. Notwithstanding

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:05.700
that, many patients in that pathway may end
up having an urgent condition. I would like

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:13.209
to remind Board members that the standard
we now have for those patients is that 92%

00:01:13.209 --> 00:01:20.300
of them will have waited less than 18 weeks.
If you remember, we reduced a number of standards

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:25.119
down to that one following on from Bruce's
review, on the grounds that it was felt that

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:31.490
it was better for patients and better to ensure
that the longest waiters were treated appropriately.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:38.000
I would also like to make the point that this
is not about waiting 18 weeks to be seen.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.119
It is about waiting 18 weeks until you get
your definitive treatment - much like the

00:01:43.119 --> 00:01:46.660
A&amp;E standard, where we often slip into saying
it is four hours to be seen. It is not; it

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:54.259
is four hours to be admitted or discharged.
This is about the full treatment. I would

00:01:54.259 --> 00:01:59.259
also like to make the point that most patients
have their definitive treatment much sooner

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:04.179
than this. The wait of 18 weeks, or those
patients who sadly wait longer than 18 weeks,

00:02:04.179 --> 00:02:12.399
tends to be for those patients who have very
complex conditions and have needed a myriad

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:19.500
of diagnostic tests and very careful consideration
before their treatment has eventually been

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.080
decided between the doctor and the patient.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:28.860
For the moment, this standard is being met,
and we continue to work hard and be optimistic

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:34.610
that it will be met throughout the year. We
have one small cautionary note, and that is

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:41.770
that the activity does appear to be rising.
We are seeing a continuing rise in GP and

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:47.380
other referrals into the system, which on
the first couple of months of data suggests

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:54.690
that activity may be slightly higher than
we have predicted as a need. I think it is

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:59.220
often quite difficult to make assumptions
when you only have two or three months of

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:03.820
totally validated activity, so this is something
that we will be keeping a very close eye on

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:10.280
to ensure that the activity is adequate and
that our partners in the trusts have the capacity

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:11.970
to do that.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:19.250
A second area that I would like to discuss
is the ambulance standards - obviously, the

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:24.340
time taken for ambulances to reach patients
who need them. These standards are very challenging.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:30.819
We have seen some improvement, but the ambulance
standards have been challenged for some while.

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:37.270
One area which we hope will make a difference
and where we will see improvements is that

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:42.620
we have some pilots running at the moment
whereby, for patients who are not in the very

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:49.900
most urgent category but the next category
down, the call handlers answering the calls

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:56.390
are given slightly longer - two minutes longer
- to decide whether and how and what ambulance

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:04.099
to dispatch to the patient. The reason for
this is that there is evidence that for that

00:04:04.099 --> 00:04:08.209
particular category of calls, many ambulances
are dispatched very quickly which are then

00:04:08.209 --> 00:04:09.660
not needed.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:14.099
Obviously that has the knock-on effect of
that ambulance not being available for another

00:04:14.099 --> 00:04:18.940
patient who might need it. What the pilots
are looking at is, number one, making absolutely

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:23.190
sure that, for those patients who fit into
the category where the ambulance is dispatched

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:31.900
slightly more slowly, no harm comes to them
if we change the standard, and similarly that

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:38.660
there is benefit to the wider patient population
from the ambulances being freed up and released.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:42.440
Those pilots are ongoing at the moment, but
the early indication is that what is coming

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:46.980
out of that is quite helpful and will prove
to be of benefit for patients.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:56.660
A third area that we are worried about and
are doing a lot of work on with our partners,

00:04:56.660 --> 00:05:02.660
particularly in the new NHS Improvement or
the overseers of the provider trusts, is the

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:09.350
standard for cancer. We meet most of the standards,
but the one we have not been meeting recently

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:19.020
is the 62-day wait. Again, that is the wait
that patients have to their definitive treatment.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:24.610
Although no one wants any cancer patient to
wait longer than they have to, clearly in

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:31.210
some instances the diagnosis and the decision
as to what the best treatment is can be quite

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:33.210
complicated.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:38.419
It is also important to remind everyone that
92% of patients who go into this pathway do

00:05:38.419 --> 00:05:44.080
not turn out to have cancer. The pathway is
designed to make sure that those patients

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:49.580
who do have cancer are treated very quickly,
but we are seeing a lot of patients on that

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:54.080
pathway who fortunately do not have cancer
and can be discharged.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:06:00.100
One of the key reasons why we are struggling
to meet the very high standards that we have

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:05.590
set ourselves in England is that activity
has gone up enormously. It is rising by approximately

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:10.350
10% per annum into this pathway, which is
higher than almost anything else we see. However,

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:15.770
that is excellent because we know that early
diagnosis of cancer is one of the ways we

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:21.669
can improve survival. As I have said to the
Board in the past, the number of initiatives

00:06:21.669 --> 00:06:27.580
that we have had to help raise awareness of
cancer is part of the reason for this rise.

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:33.400
In fact, there has been a recent campaign
for older women with breast cancer, and we

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:40.669
are beginning to see a rise in patients coming
forward on that basis. That is something that

00:06:40.669 --> 00:06:43.820
obviously we don't want to do anything about.
We want to keep the patients coming forward,

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:48.440
so we have to work extra hard to make sure
that we have got everything in place in order

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:54.680
to make sure that all patients can be seen
and treated quickly. There are some capacity

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:59.340
issues particularly around workforce, and
making sure that we have got the right trained

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:05.139
staff to deal with the growing demand, and
sometimes some of our equipment is old and

00:07:05.139 --> 00:07:12.440
needs replacing in order to deal with the
numbers and to have the most up-to-date treatments.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:16.509
If you look at what is happening, one of the
things that is apparent is that the reason

00:07:16.509 --> 00:07:22.979
for this standard being missed is the middle
portion of the pathway, which is about the

00:07:22.979 --> 00:07:27.650
time from the outpatient appointment to the
diagnosis. The amount of time the patients

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:34.259
wait to see the consultant in the first instance
is pretty static, and the amount of time once

00:07:34.259 --> 00:07:38.900
the diagnosis is made to the definitive start
of treatment is fairly static as well. Where

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:44.570
we are seeing the rise in this time is for
the diagnosis to be made from the diagnostic

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:48.500
tests. We are particularly seeing a problem
with endoscopy.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:53.150
The graph that you see in the report shows
just how many more endoscopies we need to

00:07:53.150 --> 00:08:00.270
do. Endoscopy is a test whereby you look inside
with a light. The two or three that cause

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:04.380
us the most problems are the ones where we
look down into the stomach, the ones where

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:08.580
we look up from the bottom into the colon
and rectum, and the ones where we look into

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:14.229
the bladder. We are particularly struggling
with increases in those, and particularly

00:08:14.229 --> 00:08:18.310
gastroscopy and colonoscopy.

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:24.419
Whilst we are starting with some long-term
solutions as to how we can get the right capacity

00:08:24.419 --> 00:08:29.979
in place overall, one initiative that we have
undertaken recently is to map out all the

00:08:29.979 --> 00:08:37.039
available capacity to do this endoscopy across
England, looking very closely at what is available

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:42.079
in the independent sector, but looking at
some of our own NHS hospitals who have spare

00:08:42.079 --> 00:08:43.309
capacity as well.

00:08:43.309 --> 00:08:49.399
We now have a full mapping of all that capacity
which we are sharing and working with all

00:08:49.399 --> 00:08:53.490
the provider Trusts where they are struggling,
and where it seems that they do not have the

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:58.660
right amount of capacity for those patients,
so that we can then quickly get those patients

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:04.129
transferred. They can have their tests quickly
and then get the diagnosis.

00:09:04.129 --> 00:09:10.360
The fourth and final one that I will mention
is access to psychological therapies. You

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:15.129
will notice that, while we do very well on
access and are broadly meeting the standards

00:09:15.129 --> 00:09:19.379
that have been set, we are not doing as well
in achieving the standards we have set on

00:09:19.379 --> 00:09:25.499
recovery rates. The latter is clearly the
more important one; you cannot get the latter

00:09:25.499 --> 00:09:27.139
without the former.

00:09:27.139 --> 00:09:34.920
I think that the difference is in the way
that we can deliver these. In terms of access,

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:39.499
it is simply a question of absolutely ensuring
that the right number of appointments are

00:09:39.499 --> 00:09:43.279
available, and I think the NHS has responded
really well over the last few months to put

00:09:43.279 --> 00:09:48.540
those appointments in place for patients.
I think that that rapid expansion in the number

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:53.660
of appointments has meant that in some instances
some of the services that we are getting do

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:58.699
not have quite the good results that others
do. Without doubt they will therefore need

00:09:58.699 --> 00:09:59.929
to consolidate.

00:09:59.929 --> 00:10:04.240
We need to look across at all the services
and identify which are the ones that can achieve

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:10.779
the best recovery rates, and make sure that
those are the quality standards and the kite

00:10:10.779 --> 00:10:17.209
marks of what we are actually commissioning.
I think that, although it will take slightly

00:10:17.209 --> 00:10:21.869
longer, because it is more complex and it
is about making sure the clinical pathways

00:10:21.869 --> 00:10:27.029
are right, I am confident that we will see
a rise in the recovery rates, because our

00:10:27.029 --> 00:10:31.199
clinicians across the NHS are doing a lot
of work with those areas and those services

00:10:31.199 --> 00:10:36.389
which are struggling. Chairman, I think the
rest is if anyone has any questions, either

00:10:36.389 --> 00:10:43.970
for me on most of the standards, or for Jane
or Bruce if it was the Outcomes Framework.

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:51.149
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you. Questions? I think
one of the areas that is very interesting

00:10:51.149 --> 00:10:57.519
is the views of bereaved carers on the quality
of care in the last three months of life.

00:10:57.519 --> 00:11:02.959
The trouble is, you take these aggregated
data across the whole system and they do not

00:11:02.959 --> 00:11:09.399
tell you a great deal about what happens in
individual settings. What notice is taken

00:11:09.399 --> 00:11:15.829
of those reports that come in that are less
than satisfactory? This is not an indicator

00:11:15.829 --> 00:11:23.069
that seems to be improving. How do we learn
from the data in order to try to improve the

00:11:23.069 --> 00:11:25.209
offer to those in their last months of life?

00:11:25.209 --> 00:11:29.029
&gt;&gt;Barbara Hakin: Maybe I can hand over to
Jane on that one, because as I say the outcomes

00:11:29.029 --> 00:11:31.970
framework is the one that the Clinical Directorates
oversees.

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:40.649
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: I think it is a really important
question. Certainly the care of bereaved people

00:11:40.649 --> 00:11:48.059
and actually dying is one of our key priorities,
and we have just launched some further work

00:11:48.059 --> 00:11:52.369
on end-of-life care. The patient experience
team do spend quite a lot of time concentrating

00:11:52.369 --> 00:11:57.480
on this particular field. They work with other
external organisations. We have very good

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:03.220
joint work with people like McMillan to look
at how we can support people, both at end-of-life

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:04.470
and their relatives.

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:11.160
In terms of individual people and carers'
stories, that tends to happen much more at

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:16.790
a local level. If we have access to that level
of detail then we can aggregate that and build

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:20.179
good case studies and good stories from it,
but the networks and the work we are doing

00:12:20.179 --> 00:12:25.529
at the moment is actually concentrating on
that significantly. While I am not completely

00:12:25.529 --> 00:12:30.769
surprised that the figures have not changed
significantly, and it does say very clearly

00:12:30.769 --> 00:12:37.339
that we need to do more statistical investigation
to check whether it is significant, as I have

00:12:37.339 --> 00:12:42.319
said before on many occasions, one story is
one story too many, and you can never go back

00:12:42.319 --> 00:12:47.040
and say, 'It will be okay next time.' I think
that is something that we really need to emphasise

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:55.029
and concentrate on. Carer work and bereaved
carers are absolute areas of prioritisation

00:12:55.029 --> 00:12:57.170
for the experience team.

00:12:57.170 --> 00:13:04.579
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: You mentioned a few times the
word 'struggling', Barbara, and goodness knows

00:13:04.579 --> 00:13:08.939
the system is struggling in so many different
ways. However, there is one indicator here

00:13:08.939 --> 00:13:14.309
which is buried, and I think it is interesting
to give it more spotlight; that is this patient

00:13:14.309 --> 00:13:22.079
experience indicator, which if you were in
any other walk of life you would look at as

00:13:22.079 --> 00:13:28.410
a serious measure of how well we are doing.
It looks like we are doing really well. I

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:32.839
understand that there are one or two methodological
blips in the way that we handle patient experience

00:13:32.839 --> 00:13:39.720
data, but why is that? Why is the patient
experience coming out consistently strong?

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:46.069
&gt;&gt;Barbara Hakin: I think that is a difficult
question to answer, and I suppose all I can

00:13:46.069 --> 00:13:52.499
suggest is maybe a personal view. The NHS
has consistently over decades scored very

00:13:52.499 --> 00:13:59.600
highly on patient experience compared to private
industry, and obviously that is why people

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:06.179
look at this and think, 'Gosh this is marvellous.'
My only worry about it is that I think particularly

00:14:06.179 --> 00:14:12.589
the older population, they have less expectation
of the NHS. Despite the fact that they have

00:14:12.589 --> 00:14:18.540
paid for it with many years of taxes, there
is a degree that it is not a right, that it

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:22.959
is something that they should be grateful
for the service we deliver, as opposed to

00:14:22.959 --> 00:14:27.619
if they going to a shop and buy something.
I think although it is a very high standard

00:14:27.619 --> 00:14:32.429
here, I think it is something that we need
to make sure that we are not complacent about,

00:14:32.429 --> 00:14:40.239
because patients often are not as critical
of the NHS as they would be of other services

00:14:40.239 --> 00:14:44.769
they get, because of the way they feel about
the NHS. I think that is a bit of a generational

00:14:44.769 --> 00:14:49.589
thing which we will see change as well, but
that is a personal view. They are high figures

00:14:49.589 --> 00:14:53.379
and we should be very proud of it. It is testimony
to our staff.

00:14:53.379 --> 00:14:55.170
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Yes, absolutely.

00:14:55.170 --> 00:15:00.109
&gt;&gt;Ciaran Devane: Just following on from that,
the other thing is where staff report good

00:15:00.109 --> 00:15:04.920
experience at work, patients report good experience
at work, and there is an absolutely clear

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:09.720
correlation; whether it is in a specific condition
like cancer, or it is the workforce more broadly

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:12.869
as well. So it gets back to this leadership
thing.

00:15:12.869 --> 00:15:20.160
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Just very briefly, I completely
concur with what Ciaran said. Of course, this

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:25.689
is one high level indicator; there are actually
a lot more detailed questions that sit underneath

00:15:25.689 --> 00:15:30.100
this. We know, for example, that patients
have more recently said that they feel they

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:37.569
are being treated with compassion. That actually
has gone up, which is really encouraging.

00:15:37.569 --> 00:15:42.569
If you look at GP patient experience, then
actually that is even higher. One of the things

00:15:42.569 --> 00:15:46.499
we could do if the Board thinks it would be
useful, is to maybe bring back some further

00:15:46.499 --> 00:15:50.199
information, some more detail, as we look
beneath some of these high-level ones and

00:15:50.199 --> 00:15:55.959
actually give a bit more explanation about
what we are doing, together with colleagues

00:15:55.959 --> 00:16:01.559
throughout the service about where we are.
I don't know whether that would be useful?

00:16:01.559 --> 00:16:01.899
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Yes, I think that definitely
would. Because as we know that we talked about,

00:16:01.899 --> 00:16:05.069
there is actually a huge variation between
organisations, between parts of the country

00:16:05.069 --> 00:16:11.169
and between patient groups. Even just on the
GP access one, if you look at the data that

00:16:11.169 --> 00:16:16.259
I think HSCIC just recently published, you
can see parts of the country where difficulty

00:16:16.259 --> 00:16:20.129
of getting hold of a GP appointment links
to underlying recruitment retention problems.

00:16:20.129 --> 00:16:26.160
It is all laid out there. As we talked about
previously, big differences between ethnic

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:33.619
groups as well. So, patients of Pakistani
origin are I think 10% less satisfied with

00:16:33.619 --> 00:16:39.169
the ability to access GP services. That tells
us that, if we are serious about inequalities

00:16:39.169 --> 00:16:42.519
reduction, then we have got to target those
areas and those groups in new ways.

00:16:42.519 --> 00:16:48.459
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much Barbara,
for the paper and for the report.

